h of you nice to see you  both  so first of all climate change and its  devastating effects are already here  that's the clear message that we heard  today from the president of palau a  string of pristine islands in the south  pacific  palau is home to around 18 000 people so  how can countries on the front line of  the climate crisis often tiny have a  voice in the global debate and tessa one  way that the president of palau was  telling me that he was trying to have  his voice heard was by taking legal  action joining a group of small island  states and taking a case to a u.n  tribunal how unusual is a case like that  and what might it be able to achieve  well anna these sorts of cases basically  people turning to courts to try to find  new ways to get governments to speed up  the pace and scale at which they're  reducing  carbon emissions that in itself isn't  unusual at the national level what's  unusual here is that palau and these  other pacific island states who are  facing such an imminent and massive  threat you know to their very existence  that they're collectively acting as a  set of governments to really clarify the  obligations of other countries and you  know principally richer countries with  higher historical responsibility for  climate change like the uk which is in  the top 10 countries in the world in  terms of its historical contribution to  carbon emissions you know they're  they're trying to clarify exactly what  the legal obligations are under  international law  for those countries in terms of having  to reduce emissions and support  countries like palau that are really at  the front lines of climate change of the  climate crisis despite having done so  little to contribute to it  and you can certainly understand why  they would do that given that we are  effectively still traveling in the wrong  direction in terms of reducing emissions  at the pace that we need to to stay  within a safe climate that critical 1.5  degree threshold that's especially  important for small islands and you've  got governments like the uk at the  moment considering opening up new oil  and gas fields you know at a time when  the world's energy and climate experts  couldn't be clearer that that is  incompatible with a livable climate so i  think that's that's a whole topic that  we've  talked about a few times this week and  i'm sure we'll come back to you again  another time at tessa but but dan these  these small island estates are the ones  who are bearing the brunt of climate  change at the moment and yet in the case  of palau the president was very clear as  saying i know that most people couldn't  even find it on a map so what what does  that mean for public awareness do you  think do you think that people here will  listen to those pleas or do they think  well i haven't heard of palau in some  cases what do you think how much  resonance can his message have  well my heart went out to him i was  listening to that very kind of moving  clip and unfortunately we lack empathy  as a species we know we know that um and  it's very much a case of out of sight  out of mind with these things i mean i  think that um in world war ii there was  um twinning of cities and i think i  wonder if you know places like palau  should be twinned with places like  portsmouth and they should have like  digital communication so people can see  on the ground here in the uk what's  going on elsewhere otherwise it's just  too remote it's just too big to tackle  these people going through things right  now and we need to kind of raise  awareness of that  and tessa how important is it to get  public opinion on board elsewhere in the  world and can legal action make a  difference in that  well i think you know we should be clear  that even though the uk isn't quite as  vulnerable as a country like palau the  uk is certainly  not immune to the impacts of the climate  crisis there are already tens of  thousands of people and businesses whose  you know people whose homes have been  destroyed by flooding who have had to  relocate in recent years as rainfall  becomes more intense and more frequent  in some parts of the country so i don't  think we need to look elsewhere to  really appreciate what the climate  crisis means for all of us um but legal  action like the one that the government  of palau is is pursuing is certainly a  way i think to really clearly tell a  story about the fact that governments  have been making promises for decades  now  at the international level under these  u.n climate agreements and so on to take  action on climate change and those  promises have for the most part gone  fulfilled and to support vulnerable  countries like palau to adapt to and  cope with the impacts of climate change  so i think it's really important that  people understand that this isn't you  know something new this is something  that governments have been promising and  failing to address now for many years  dan quickly want to bring you in just  just the thought that do you think that  ultimately people are only really going  to take action when their own basement's  being flooded rather than seeing  somebody from a far  far away land talking about their issues  i hope not but the cynic in me says that  that could be the case i think we just  need you know platforms like sky to  raise the issue more and more um people  have got to kind of realize that every  action in this part of the world has  effects elsewhere and you know  as you just heard things are happening  here now as well so i really hope people  start to wake up to this and get the  message okay let's move on to our next  topic shall we because the uk's uh  climate change committee is the  government's official advisory body on  climate policy so comments by its chief  chris stark might get some traction now  he said in an interview that the  government's policy on home insulation  is very poor and he says it's insulation  as well as renewable power which is  holding the key to solving the current  energy crisis and dan criticism of the  government's policy then on insulation  and do you agree then with chris stark  that  it's a way out of the current energy  crisis good for people's bills good for  the planet  100 i think chris is actually quite  moderate in his criticism of the  government on this you know they have  consistently failed  on insulation uh you don't necessarily  need to look at an energy form  certificate of your house to know if it  is cold or drafty there's much more that  you can do  but the government needs to step up to  the plate on uh insulation what's quite  often conflated is electricity and heat  and heating in hot water is  the larger portion of your uh your bill  and if you're  you know burning fossil fuels in your  house the gas boiler or something uh  similar you know it's absolutely galling  to see that heat leave through doors  floors uh windows and chimneys and you  know you can  get your house insulated but even before  that i would strongly suggest working  out all the weather weaknesses the weak  points are in your house and just  employing draft exclusion for example  well yes some really good tips and tests  the thing is that people might even be  aware that if they improve their  insulation they can save a bit of money  but often it  involves an upfront cost doesn't it and  people are facing a squeeze on their  wallets already  yeah that's exactly right i mean the  cost of our current dependency on both  gas to heat our homes the failure of the  uk government especially when you  compare it to how quickly  other european governments for example  have managed to insulate and make homes  more  more energy efficient you know it's  really clear that this is a political  choice that the government has made not  to prioritize not just the climate cost  of continuing to  burn fossil fuels to heat our homes but  also the costs as you say you know to  people's bills which are being driven by  our dependency on gas and to people's  health you know and the quality of life  that people are really going to have to  compromise as they try and reduce and  conserve their energy use just so that  they don't get stuck with these  completely you know unimaginably  difficult  energy bills at the end of the month so  you know the costs of doing this have  not the cost of failing to really  take the way in which the uk's housing  stock is dependent on fossil fuels and  ultimately not consistent with what  people need to lead a comfortable life  is incredibly high yeah and the  government obviously would talk about  all its um energy that it's putting into  renewables at the moment it would point  out that it's a leader in in wind power  for example and that it would say it's  got plans for trying to make sure that  our our homes are more energy efficient  as well  dan practically people often don't know  where to start do they when they're um  looking to insulate their home or do  other energy efficient measures have you  got any particular tips that people  should try out so certainly draft  exclusion would be a key thing but there  are other things you can do you know  short shower is much better than a deep  bath in terms of the amount of energy  you use to heat up that water things  like aerated shower heads are relatively  cheap and easy to install uh smart  thermostats which means you don't have  to have your heating on in every room  you can have it in the rooms where you  need it and have clever things uh to to  make sure you're being as energy  efficient as possible and to be honest  this problem is not going away in fact  i'd urge people to spend the spring and  summer when things get a bit warmer  warmer to start planning for autumn  and winter the the gas  cost of gas is is going to potentially  get worse we've heard that  ultimately boiler replacements will get  cheaper things like air source heat  pumps have a huge role to play and there  are companies working really hard to get  the cost of those uh down to a  comparable level but ultimately there  are things you can do that that are that  are cheaper um and yeah i'd suggest you  know let's get ready for next winter  okay so some good tips there well tessa  khan and dan caesar very interesting to  get your views and your advice thanks  very much indeed  so that's it from me for today but  tomorrow i'll be joined by climate  activist laura young and michael jacobs  professor of political economy at the  university of sheffield but that's it  for now thanks for watching see you next  time  [Music]
 

